WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.740 --> 00:00:04.270
Lina runs customer service
for a fictional bakery chain.

2
00:00:04.270 --> 00:00:06.880
She watches an AI assistant demo.

3
00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:09.190
Every answer is quick and polite.

4
00:00:09.330 --> 00:00:12.300
Then a messier fictional request arrives.

5
00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:16.580
A customer wants to change part of
an order, and mentions an allergy.

6
00:00:16.580 --> 00:00:20.435
The assistant answers confidently,
and misses the allergy.

7
00:00:20.575 --> 00:00:22.815
The demo used easy questions.

8
00:00:22.815 --> 00:00:26.153
Nobody had written down what
a good answer must include.

9
00:00:26.293 --> 00:00:29.813
OpenAI’s guide to evaluation starts there:

10
00:00:29.813 --> 00:00:32.373
decide the success criteria for your task,

11
00:00:32.373 --> 00:00:34.143
before you judge any model.

12
00:00:34.283 --> 00:00:36.713
Then build a set of example cases.

13
00:00:36.713 --> 00:00:39.350
The guide says to include typical cases,

14
00:00:39.350 --> 00:00:42.073
edge cases and adversarial cases.

15
00:00:42.213 --> 00:00:45.973
In Lina’s test kitchen,
ordinary orders run smoothly.

16
00:00:45.973 --> 00:00:49.560
An edge case, like a half-changed
order, slows the line.

17
00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:51.190
A tricky one stops it.

18
00:00:51.330 --> 00:00:53.710
Keep a separate set of cases for checking,

19
00:00:53.710 --> 00:00:56.260
so you aren’t only testing
what you tuned for.

20
00:00:56.400 --> 00:00:59.620
Review real outputs
against a clear rubric.

21
00:00:59.620 --> 00:01:04.170
The guide recommends detailed human
review to refine that scorecard.

22
00:01:04.310 --> 00:01:06.630
Compare options on the same cases,

23
00:01:06.630 --> 00:01:09.329
and run the checks again after every change.

24
00:01:09.469 --> 00:01:13.839
No finite set of tests proves an
assistant will always be right.

25
00:01:13.839 --> 00:01:17.619
But it can show you where it
fails, before a customer finds out.

26
00:01:17.759 --> 00:01:20.429
Before you trust a demo, ask three things.

27
00:01:20.429 --> 00:01:22.089
Which cases were tested?

28
00:01:22.089 --> 00:01:23.639
What counted as success?

29
00:01:23.639 --> 00:01:25.629
And what evidence shows it passed?

30
00:01:25.769 --> 00:01:28.119
A smooth demo is a first impression.

31
00:01:28.119 --> 00:01:30.276
A tested case is evidence.

32
00:01:30.416 --> 00:01:31.886
Ahmadreza Samadi.

33
00:01:31.886 --> 00:01:33.823
